The one true awk on Fuzix
=========================

Lucent's awk (github.com/onetrueawk/awk), the maintained descendant of
the V7 original by one of its authors.  8,157 lines of C under the
permissive licence in LICENSE.

WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM UPSTREAM
-------------------------------

Deliberately almost nothing, so that taking a newer awk is a copy
rather than a merge.  Two files differ, by three lines between them:

  awk.h     one #include of "fuzix_compat.h", and an #ifndef around
            the NCHARS definition so that header can set it.

Everything else this platform needs is in files upstream does not have:

  fuzix_compat.h   MB_CUR_MAX, NCHARS, random/srandom, FOPEN_MAX,
                   mbtowc/wctomb
  fuzix_wctype.c   towupper/towlower

The generated files are vendored rather than built: awkgram.tab.c and
awkgram.tab.h come from yacc, proctab.c from running maketab over the
latter.  Both are host tools, so neither this build nor the board needs
yacc.  `make -f Makefile.armm0 tables' regenerates them if awkgram.y is
ever touched.  byacc was used rather than bison, which upstream's own
makefile prefers; it reports 44 shift/reduce and 85 reduce/reduce
conflicts, which look alarming and are inherent to the grammar - the
resulting parser was checked against user-defined functions, dynamic
regexes, getline in each form, pipes and printf, and behaves the same.

THE DECISIONS, AND WHY
----------------------

MB_CUR_MAX is 1.  Fuzix has one locale, C, and no wide-character
support.  This is not a stub being papered over: awk carries
awk_mb_cur_max, initialised to 1, and every hot path in b.c and run.c
reads `if (c < 128 || awk_mb_cur_max == 1)'.  Setting it selects a path
upstream maintains.  UTF-8 input passes through as bytes, so
length("e-acute") is 2 rather than 1 and substr can split a character.
On a machine whose console font is 8x12 ASCII that is the honest answer.

NCHARS is 256+3, not 1256+3.  That is the width of one row of the
regular expression DFA, and upstream sizes it for Unicode runes.  Each
state callocs NCHARS entries of eight bytes and resize_state takes ten
states at a time, so the first growth of any DFA asks for about a
hundred kilobytes at once - which failed on the board, and awk reported
"regular expression too big" for gsub(/o/, "0", s).  With
awk_mb_cur_max at 1 the matcher only ever indexes a row by a byte, so
the thousand entries above 255 cannot be reached.  A row goes from
10,072 bytes to 2,072 and nothing is lost.

random() and srandom() become lrand48() and srand48().  An exact
substitution, not an approximation: both give [0, 2^31-1], and run.c
divides by 0x80000000 rather than by RAND_MAX.  rand() would have been
the obvious choice and the wrong one - RAND_MAX is 32767 here, so awk's
rand() would have had 32768 distinct values.

FOPEN_MAX is 10.  Only the initial size of awk's own table of
redirections, which run.c reallocs; the real ceiling is the kernel's
UFTSIZE of ten files per process, three already spent on stdin, stdout
and stderr.

towupper/towlower are defined rather than macro'd.  They are passed to
nawk_convert as function pointers.  awk carries its own for DJGPP,
which lacks them for the same reason, but those are static and collide
with the declaration the toolchain's wctype.h already provides - so the
definitions live in fuzix_wctype.c.

WHAT THE C LIBRARY NEEDED
-------------------------

Three real gaps, all fixed in Library rather than worked around here,
and all of them things other programs will want:

  stdlib.h        system() had been in the library and undeclared
                  since it was written.

  Makefile.armm0  drand48.c and lrand48.c existed and were never
                  compiled, so every one of the seven functions
                  stdlib.h declares was an undefined reference.

  fnum.c          NEW.  vfprintf.c has called _fnum since it was
                  written and nothing ever defined it, so the %e/%f/%g
                  cases sat behind BUILD_LIBM, which no armm0 build
                  defined - and a %f on this machine printed the
                  letter f.  awk made it impossible to miss: awk
                  converts every number with CONVFMT, "%.6g", so
                  `print 3.5' produced `g'.

  vfprintf.c      %j (intmax_t) and %z (size_t).  awk emits %jd and
                  %jx for every integer conversion a program writes,
                  so printf("%x", 255) came out as "jx".

WHAT WAS CHECKED, ON THE BOARD
------------------------------

Built by the cross compiler, run on a PC3 from /bin/awk:

  associative arrays and for-in       user-defined functions
  dynamic regexes from -v             getline < file
  output pipes to a command           split, substr, index
  field assignment and NF             sqrt, exp, log, int
  rand/srand spread                   gsub, toupper, length
  printf widths, precision, %x, %d    NR and pattern counting
  integers printing as integers

_fnum itself was checked against glibc's printf over 13,596 cases
before it went near the board - see the note at the top of fnum.c.
